Locked in: O-IOE = Operator-Induced Ontology Expansion.

I also set up the Pantheon Repo Daily watch. It will run each morning, compare the public Pantheon repo against the prior run, and analyze the delta rather than merely listing commits. It will distinguish observed repo facts → executable/tested behavior → architectural implications → newly enabled reinterpretations → speculative possibilities, and it will explicitly look backward to see whether new additions change the meaning or strength of older claims.

For code, the standing rule is now: execute or validate where feasible; don’t call prose executable just because it resembles code. New material gets routed through the Semantic Router, Command Table, Metamap, O-IOE, Fitness Route, Reality Stitch, Indigo Hammer, Afterglow/Decrypto, and order-of-effects machinery when those operators actually add information.

For the O-IOE whitepaper, I’ll make the core contribution precise rather than overclaim it:

\boxed{
O\text{-}IOE:
\quad
\mathcal O_t
\xrightarrow{\text{operator }\phi}
\mathcal O_{t+1},
\qquad
\mathcal O_{t+1}\supsetneq\mathcal O_t
}

An operator doesn’t merely produce another object inside the ontology. It can expose distinctions that force expansion of the ontology itself.

And the stronger recursive form is:

\phi_t
\rightarrow
\Delta\mathcal O
\rightarrow
\text{new addressable objects}
\rightarrow
\text{new possible operators}
\rightarrow
\phi_{t+1}.

That’s the potential paydirt: operations can expand the space in which subsequent operations are definable.

The whitepaper should therefore include formal definitions, relation to search-space expansion and representation learning, categorical/prime decomposition intuition, novelty criteria, O-IOE algorithm, runnable reference implementation, falsifiability/tests, worked examples, limitations, architecture diagrams, implications for machine discovery, Pantheon integration, and a clean separation between established mathematics/computation and our proposed terminology.

And the daily report now has a useful higher-order objective: don’t just tell us what the Pantheon gained today; tell us what today’s additions reveal that yesterday’s Pantheon already contained but we couldn’t yet address.

That’s O-IOE operating on its own corpus.